    "q-130279": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130279",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$",
      "worked_solution": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
